I applied the design-taste skill. Since I can't write files this time, this is the plan only. Nothing was rendered or checked in a browser.

**1. Design read**
A plain, high-contrast product page. The type is large, and the set and its glaze are drawn in code in the colours of Porto. That is because there are no product photos, and the strongest true fact about the set is that every one comes out of the kiln different.

**2. Idea sentence**
On this page, **the hand-mixed glaze of each Porto batch** becomes **the page's colour field and its one live thing**. Each visit draws the set in a different glaze, and the hero, the glaze section and the footer all take on that colour. A "Mix another glaze" button pours a new one. Without script, the page shows one fixed glaze.

The glazes are named and coloured after Porto: azulejo cobalt, Douro green, granite, Ribeira ochre. That passes the competitor test, because another ceramics brand can't claim these.

**3. Sections**
1. **Hero:** split screen. On the left: wordmark, headline, supporting sentence and a "Buy the set — €84" button. On the right half: the four pieces drawn as flat shapes on a full field of today's glaze, with the glaze's name and "Mix another glaze" underneath.
2. **The set:** one row across the full width with all four pieces drawn to their real relative size. Each piece's name, size and capacity sits under it in plain text. It reads as a line-up, not cards.
3. **The glaze:** the whole section is filled with the current glaze colour. It holds a large paragraph on why no two sets match, plus a strip of six earlier batches drawn as glaze runs and named after Porto streets.
4. **Porto:** one large credited archive photograph of the city (Ribeira or an azulejo front, from Wikimedia Commons). Beside it, a short text on where and how the sets are glazed. It shows the city, never the product.
5. **Order:** €84 at display size, what's in the box, "Ships across the EU within a week", and the button. Next to it, two columns of short questions and answers: filter size, dishwasher, returns, "will mine match the picture?"
6. **Footer:** KILN in the glaze colour across the full width, with the photo credits and a contact line.

**4. First screen**
- Headline: **A pour-over set glazed in Porto** (31 characters, so it stays within two lines at any width)
- Supporting sentence: **A dripper, a carafe and two cups, glazed by hand in small batches, so no two sets are alike. €84, shipped across the EU within a week.**
- Button: **Buy the set — €84**. It points to `#checkout` and has to be connected to real checkout before launch.

**5. CSS custom properties**
```css
:root {
  /* colour roles */
  --ground: #ECEBE7;        /* unglazed stoneware, cool off-white */
  --ground-step: #E1DFDA;   /* tone step between surfaces */
  --ink: #1B1E21;           /* iron glaze, all running text */
  --ink-soft: #4A4F55;      /* secondary notes only */
  --accent: #1E4DA6;        /* azulejo cobalt: the buy button, focus */
  --on-accent: #FFFFFF;
  --glaze: #2F5D50;         /* live field, reset per visit by JS */
  --on-glaze: #F4F3EF;
  /* glaze set the JS picks from (each passes contrast with --on-glaze) */
  --glaze-cobalt: #1E4DA6;
  --glaze-douro: #2F5D50;
  --glaze-granite: #55585B;
  --glaze-ribeira: #8A5A1C;

  /* type roles: one family */
  --font: "Schibsted Grotesk", system-ui, sans-serif;
  --size-display: clamp(2.75rem, 8.4vw, 8.5rem);
  --size-statement: clamp(1.5rem, 2.6vw, 2.25rem);
  --size-text: 1.125rem;
  --size-note: 0.9375rem;
  --track-display: -0.035em;
  --lead-display: 0.92;
  --lead-text: 1.5;
  --weight-display: 700;
  --weight-text: 400;

  --margin: clamp(16px, 2.2vw, 32px);
  --section-gap: clamp(96px, 12vw, 200px);
  --radius: 0;              /* square buttons and media */
}
```

**Still to confirm before launch:**
- **Sample details:** the piece sizes and capacities, the batch street names and the Porto archive photo are placeholders I'd fill in. They need checking or sourcing.
- **Drawings:** the product drawings stand in until you shoot photos.

